# Introduction {#intro}

The Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) enables independently administered Autonomous Systems (ASes) to exchange reachability information {{?RFC4271}}. Each AS determines its routing behavior through local policies, operational procedures, topology, and configuration. An AS may have expectations about how it will originate, accept, select, advertise, or propagate routes for a particular inter-domain connection. These expectations are normally implemented through local routing policy and operational controls.

Intent-Based Networking (IBN) provides a general approach for expressing network goals at an appropriate level of abstraction and translating those goals into network actions. IBN research also considers monitoring and assurance to assess whether observed network behavior remains consistent with an intent {{?RFC9315}}. Intent classification and scope are further discussed in {{?RFC9316}}. These concepts provide an important foundation for this work.

NMRG work has considered IBN for routing and path selection, as well as IBN for interconnection. The current IBN use-case document describes routing-oriented examples and an interconnection scenario in which one provider specifies target resources, another provider establishes or provisions the requested resources, and monitoring data is used during assurance {{?I-D.irtf-nmrg-ibn-usecases}}. Earlier individual work on interconnection intents also considered conventional IP peering, interconnection expectations, targets, conditions, and context {{?I-D.contreras-nmrg-interconnection-intents}}.
This document does not claim that IBN lacks routing intent, inter-domain intent, or intent assurance. Instead, it examines a more specific question within that research area:

> How can an AS make an attributable declaration about its own expected BGP routing behavior, scoped to defined inter-domain connections and route classes, so that another independently administered domain can understand the declaration and assess it against suitable routing evidence?
A general network intent and a routing behavior commitment serve different purposes. An intent typically expresses a desired outcome or goal to be achieved by a network or service. A routing behavior commitment is a more explicit, operator-attributable statement about behavior for which a particular AS or routing domain takes responsibility. Such a commitment may be derived from, associated with, or used by an IBN system, but it is not intended to redefine the general meaning, taxonomy, lifecycle, or architecture of IBN.

The distinction is important in inter-domain environments. The AS that makes a commitment, the AS that consumes it, and the entity that observes routing behavior may be in different administrative domains. A remote observer may not have access to the issuer's internal policy configuration, complete routing information bases, or all Adj-RIB-Out state. Consequently, a commitment must identify the behavior being claimed and its scope, while an assessment must state what the available evidence can and cannot establish.
 
This document uses the term inter-domain routing behavior commitment (IRBC) for the proposed declaration model. An IRBC is issued by an AS or an authority acting on its behalf and describes commitments about that issuer's own routing behavior. It does not allow one AS to make commitments on behalf of another AS. A bilateral interconnection may involve separate declarations from both parties; one party's declaration is not evidence of the other party's agreement.
This document is a problem statement and requirements document for research in the NMRG context. It does not define an IBN system, a generic intent language, a routing-policy language, a BGP protocol extension, or an RPKI-based authorization mechanism.


# Conventions and Definitions

{::boilerplate bcp14-tagged}

# Terminology

## Administrative Domain

An **administrative domain** is a network or collection of network resources operated under a common administrative authority. An administrative domain may operate one or more ASes; the relationship between an administrative domain and an AS is not necessarily one-to-one.

## Inter-Domain Connection

An **inter-domain connection** is a routing relationship between two independently administered domains. It may be implemented by one or more eBGP sessions and may cover different address families, route classes, or operational arrangements.

## Routing Intent

A **routing intent** is an operator- or service-level expression of a desired routing outcome or goal. The term is used consistently with the general IBN concepts in {{?RFC9315}} and {{?RFC9316}}. This document does not redefine routing intent as a new IBN category.

## Routing Policy

A **routing policy** is a set of rules or configuration constructs that control how routes are accepted, selected, modified, or advertised. Policies may be represented using local configuration, policy languages, or data models.

## Inter-Domain Routing Behavior Commitment (IRBC)

An **IRBC** is an operator-authorized and issuer-attributable declaration in which an AS or routing domain states the routing behavior it undertakes to provide or enforce for specified inter-domain connections, route scopes, directions, and operating conditions. Its semantics have three related elements: (1) the subject's **connection role** in the specified inter-domain relationship; (2) the associated **routing behavior**; and (3) the **commitment context**, which defines the applicable scope, direction, conditions, exceptions, validity, and responsibility. In this document, "commitment" denotes an attributable operational statement of responsibility; it does not, by itself, create a legal obligation, demonstrate that the behavior has been followed, or establish a bilateral agreement.

## Issuer and Subject

The **issuer** is the entity that creates and publishes or otherwise provides an IRBC. The **subject** is the AS or routing domain whose behavior is described. The issuer may be the subject itself or an entity authorized to act on its behalf. A future model needs to distinguish authority over an AS number, authority over address resources, and operational authority over routing policy where those authorities differ.

## IRBC Scope

The **IRBC scope** identifies where and when a declaration applies. Relevant dimensions may include the subject AS, a neighboring AS or connection, AFI/SAFI, prefixes or route sets, origin AS, route class, direction, time interval, and operational conditions.

## Connection Role

A **connection role** describes the functional relationship or responsibility of the subject AS with respect to a specified neighboring AS or inter-domain connection. A role is relative to the connection and may be scoped by route class or operational condition. It is not necessarily a global property of an AS and is not limited to the finite BGP Role values defined in {{?RFC9234}}.

## Declared Behavior

**Declared behavior** is the routing behavior specified by an IRBC. A declaration may express a required behavior, a prohibited behavior, a permission, or a preference. These categories have different semantics and must not be treated as interchangeable. The connection role provides context for the behavior but does not, by itself, determine all behavior semantics.

## Commitment Context

The **commitment context** describes the conditions under which declared behavior applies. It may include route scope, neighboring AS, direction, AFI/SAFI, time interval, operational state, exceptions, and precedence among applicable statements. It does not imply a legal contract or a reciprocal commitment by another AS.

## Observed Behavior

**Observed behavior** is information about routing activity or state obtained at a particular observation point and time. Examples include BGP UPDATE messages, routing information base (RIB) data, BMP feeds, and other operational evidence.

## Evidence

**Evidence** is information used to support or refute a claim about observed routing behavior. Evidence has provenance, collection time, observation scope, and limitations. The absence of an observation is not necessarily evidence that an event did not occur.

## External Assessment

**External assessment** is an evaluation of an IRBC by an entity that is independent of, or outside the administrative control of, the issuer. An assessment may report that available evidence supports compliance, supports a violation, or is insufficient to reach either conclusion.

# Inter-Domain Routing Scenarios

The following scenarios illustrate the intended problem scope. They are not claims that existing mechanisms cannot implement the underlying behavior. Each scenario distinguishes local policy enforcement from an externally assessable declaration.

## Route-Class-Specific Export Commitment

AS A has an eBGP connection with AS B and applies different export behavior to different route classes. For example, customer routes may be exported to B, while routes learned from another provider are not exported to B, except under a defined operational condition. AS A can implement this behavior using local import and export policy.

The research question is whether AS A can provide an attributable declaration that identifies the route classes, neighbor, direction, exceptions, and conditions covered by its commitment. An external assessor would then need evidence from appropriate observation points to evaluate observed advertisements. A collector that does not observe an advertisement cannot, by that fact alone, prove that AS A did not advertise the route elsewhere.

## Conditional Backup Advertisement

AS A declares that it will advertise a specified route class to backup neighbor AS C only when a defined primary-path condition is met. The commitment must distinguish a mandatory condition from a preference and identify the time interval and routing scope to which it applies.

An external assessment requires evidence about both the advertisement to AS C and the condition that is claimed to trigger it. A BGP UPDATE observed at one collector may establish that an advertisement occurred at that observation point. It may not establish the complete state of AS A's internal route selection or prove that the primary path was unavailable. The assessment model must state when the evidence is sufficient and when the result is indeterminate.

## Complex or Scope-Dependent Interconnection Behavior

Two ASes may apply different routing arrangements to different route classes over what is operationally viewed as the same interconnection. RFC 9234 defines a finite set of BGP Roles and specifies behavior for those roles, including treatment of complex peering relationships {{?RFC9234}}. IRBC must not redefine or bypass these protocol roles. It may investigate whether additional, route-scope-specific behavior declarations are useful where the operators' expectations cannot be represented solely by a single session-level role and the specific OTC rules.

Any such use case must identify the exact behavior that remains to be expressed and assessed. It is not sufficient to assert that a complex relationship exists; the declaration and evidence requirements must be stated precisely.

## Control-Plane and Data-Plane Boundary

An operator may state an expectation that traffic should traverse a preferred provider or path. However, an AS_PATH observed in the BGP control plane does not necessarily establish the actual data-plane forwarding path. Internal routing, tunnels, policy-based forwarding, ECMP, and other mechanisms may affect packet forwarding.

The initial IRBC research scope therefore focuses on BGP control-plane behavior, such as route origination, advertisement, propagation, and selected attribute handling. Data-plane commitments are outside the initial scope unless the required data-plane evidence and observation model are separately defined.

# Problem Statement

## From Local Policy to an Attributable External Declaration

An AS can implement routing behavior using local policy, but that configuration does not automatically provide another domain with an interoperable statement of the behavior the AS claims it will perform. Conversely, a published statement does not demonstrate that the AS follows it. A research model is needed to distinguish internal policy, an externally understandable commitment, and evidence of actual behavior.

## Issuer, Subject, and Authority

A declaration can be assessed only if the assessor can identify the issuer, the AS or routing domain that is the subject, and the authority under which the declaration was made. The relevant authority may concern operation of the AS, address resources, or control of routing policy; these do not necessarily belong to the same entity. A general model must not assume that possession of an ASN or a prefix resource automatically proves authority to make every routing-policy commitment for an AS.

## Scope and Behavioral Semantics

A statement such as "do not export this route" is incomplete without identifying the route, neighbor or neighbor class, address family, direction, time interval, and applicable conditions. A commitment may express a mandatory behavior, a prohibition, a permission, or a preference. These meanings must be distinguishable. The model must also address overlapping scopes, exceptions, and conflicting statements.

## Evidence and External Observability

A commitment does not create the evidence required to assess it. A remote observer may have access to public BGP data, selected route collectors, a limited set of RIBs, BMP feeds, or evidence voluntarily supplied by the issuer. These sources have different coverage and trust properties. The model must relate each behavior claim to the evidence needed to assess it and must represent observation points, time intervals, provenance, coverage, and limitations.

In particular, evidence that a route was observed at one point does not prove that it was or was not advertised to every other neighbor. A missing observation must not automatically be interpreted as proof that an event did not occur.

## Unilateral and Bilateral Statements

An AS may make a unilateral declaration about its own behavior. A bilateral interconnection arrangement may require corresponding declarations or an explicit agreement by both parties. A declaration from AS A cannot establish consent or a commitment by AS B. A model must preserve the identity and authority of each issuer and provide a way to represent disagreement or inconsistent declarations without silently treating one party's statement as authoritative for the other.

## Assessment and Uncertainty

The available evidence may support compliance, support a violation, or be insufficient to reach either conclusion. A binary result is therefore not always appropriate. An assessment model should distinguish:

- **Compliant**: the available evidence supports that the declared behavior was followed within the stated scope and assessment conditions;
- **Violation**: the available evidence supports that the declared behavior was not followed within the stated scope and assessment conditions; and
- **Indeterminate**: the evidence is insufficient, incomplete, conflicting, or inapplicable to reach either conclusion.

These outcomes describe assessment confidence and evidence sufficiency; they do not imply that every routing behavior can be independently verified in all deployments.

## Lifecycle and Information Disclosure

Routing policies and inter-domain relationships change. A declaration may be updated, superseded, withdrawn, or become inapplicable when a connection or route scope changes. An assessor may retain stale or delayed information. A model must address validity, versioning, update, withdrawal, revocation, and the relationship between an observation interval and the applicable declaration version.

At the same time, publishing a commitment may reveal sensitive information about topology, customers, peering, or traffic engineering. The model should identify the minimum information required for a meaningful assessment and the limits imposed by selective disclosure.

